  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "For a particular geometric sequence of positive terms, $a_2 = 8$ and $a_6 = 648$. Find the smallest value for $n$ for which $a_n > 100\\,000$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n = 11$.\n\nUse $r = 3$; $a_{10} = 52\\,488$ and $a_{11} = 157\\,464$.",
      "worked": "Using $a_n = ar^{n-1}$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nr^4 &= \\frac{a_6}{a_2} \\\\\nr^4 &= \\frac{648}{8} \\\\\nr^4 &= 81 \\\\\nr &= 3\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe terms are positive, so the common ratio is positive. Hence,\n$$\\begin{align*}\na_n &= a_2r^{n-2} \\\\\na_n &= 8\\cdot 3^{n-2} \\\\\na_{10} &= 8\\cdot 3^8 = 52\\,488 \\\\\na_{11} &= 8\\cdot 3^9 = 157\\,464\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe sequence is increasing and $a_{10} < 100\\,000 < a_{11}$, so the smallest value is $n = 11$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
